Voice over the bridge, because fetch() to a custom scheme is blocked
===============================================================

Dictation and read-aloud never worked in the window. The engine side was fine — `quixi --transcript`
prints the live transcript, `--say` synthesises real audio — but the UI could not reach it.

The three voice calls were the ONLY relative `fetch()` in the whole interface:

    fetch("/api/quixi/voice/status")
    fetch("/api/quixi/voice/transcript")
    fetch("/api/quixi/voice/speak")

Everything else in the app talks over the EIPC bridge (`rpc(service, member, args)`), because the
webview is served from an `app://` custom scheme and WebKitGTK does not allow `fetch()` to a custom
scheme unless it is registered CORS-enabled — which it is not. So `/api/quixi/voice/status` never
answered, `engineVoice.stt` stayed false, and the Dictate button took the "this webview provides no
speech recognition, use your system dictation" branch. That dialog is written for macOS and Windows,
and on Linux it is simply false: the engine owns the microphone.

Two changes:

1. `Voice.getStatus`, `Voice.getTranscript` and `Voice.speak` are EIPC members now, served by the same
   bridge as everything else. The HTTP routes stay for other clients and for the edge API.
2. Dictate no longer waits for a probe before choosing a path. If the webview has no recogniser —
   which on WebKitGTK is always — it goes straight to the engine. If the transcript call reports an
   error, the reason is shown once instead of being swallowed, so a dead service says so.

Verified by building the workspace and by `quixi --transcript` returning the live transcript.

